  "history": "NH 48 links Delhi and Chennai the long way round, sweeping down India's west coast and across the south through Jaipur, Ahmedabad, Mumbai, Pune and Bengaluru. It was created in the 2010 renumbering by joining two of India's most famous roads: the old NH 8 from Delhi to Mumbai and the old NH 4 from Mumbai to Chennai. Together these form the western and southern sides of the Golden Quadrilateral, the four-sided network that connects India's four biggest metro cities.",
  "facts": [
    "About 2,807 km long, passing through seven states and union territories.",
    "Carries two full sides of the Golden Quadrilateral: Delhi–Mumbai and Mumbai–Chennai.",
    "Connects four of India's largest cities — Delhi, Mumbai, Bengaluru and Chennai — plus Jaipur, Ahmedabad, Surat and Pune.",
    "The Delhi–Gurugram stretch is one of the busiest roads in India.",
    "Between Mumbai and Pune it is paralleled by the Mumbai–Pune Expressway, India's first six-lane concrete expressway."
  ],
